      "patriarchy likes to pass itself off as the natural order, a fixed ladder that rewards whoever is at the top. but it costs almost everyone something, including the people it is supposed to favour. this essay looks at how it boxes men in as much as it holds women back: two sets of chains, running side by side.",
      "the usual picture of patriarchy is male dominance and female subordination. that's right about the power, but it leaves something out: patriarchy asks sacrifices of everyone under it. men are told they're on top, and what comes with that is constant pressure to perform, conform, and win.",
      "picture a boy who learns that crying is weak. a young man who finds that being vulnerable gets him laughed at or dropped. a father who can't show tenderness without covering it with stoicism. from early on, boys are taught to cut themselves off from their feelings, to distrust them, and to treat being sensitive as a defect.",
      "the result is men who keep people out. feelings get buried and never talked about. being open becomes a liability, and it's replaced with acting invulnerable, which is exhausting and keeps real closeness away.",
      "the numbers are hard to ignore: high rates of male suicide across cultures, substance use as self-medication, and violence as the one acceptable way to show distress. many of these men believed connection was weakness and asking for help was surrender. these aren't freak accidents. they follow from what patriarchy teaches.",
      "## performance anxiety (no, not like that)",
      "patriarchy demands that men keep performing, whether through achievement, control, dominance, providing, or protecting. it measures a man by how much power he can show and how many of its symbols he collects, not by his humanity. his value depends on what he can do, provide, or conquer instead of who he is. that leaves a lingering fear that under the performance there's nothing there, and that he's only as worthy as his latest win.",
      "when your identity rests on achievement, failure isn't just a setback; it threatens who you are. every challenge to your status and every ordinary human limit becomes a possible identity crisis. the man patriarchy makes is always on guard against his own humanity, because his needs and limits all threaten his shaky claim to manhood.",
      "the burden follows men for life: boys competing for dominance in schoolyards, teenagers measuring themselves by sexual conquest, adults giving up health and relationships for career advancement, and older men in crisis when retirement or physical change interrupts the performance. the patriarchal man never just is; he always has to prove.",
      "## the poverty of connection",
      "most of all, patriarchy leaves men short on real connection. the same system that teaches men to dominate teaches them to distrust intimacy and fear the openness that closeness needs. men learn to relate through competition, hierarchy, and transaction instead of mutual vulnerability and just being there.",
      "you can see it in friendships built around activities instead of talking, in romantic relationships with unequal emotional work, and in parent-child bonds held back by restraint. the patriarch ends up alone at the top, in charge of everything and connected to none of what matters.",
      "it gets worse in a crisis or in old age, when connection stops being nice to have and becomes necessary to survive. how many men face illness, grief, or big life changes with no one to lean on? how many die earlier than they had to because they couldn't make themselves see a doctor, admit they were struggling, or ask for help? the stoic self-reliance patriarchy praises turns into a trap.",
      "patriarchy justifies its demands by promising protection through power, security through dominance, and safety through strength. obviously, that promise is false. acting invulnerable doesn't get rid of vulnerability; it hides it, often from the man himself. chasing power through domination brings constant insecurity, since he always has to defend his position.",
